 ## The 3-strike rule
 
-**If you've tried 3+ fixes and it's still broken: STOP and question the architecture.** The bug may be a design problem, not a code problem. "One more fix attempt" after two failures is a red flag — escalate, rethink, or hand to a fresh agent. For a stubborn or flaky bug, have separate agents form hypotheses from disjoint evidence (logs, code, data) and put each in front of a refuter; one context tends to favour its own first theory.
+**If you've tried three or more fixes and it's still broken, stop and question the architecture.** The bug may be a design problem, not a code problem. "One more fix attempt" after two failures is a red flag — escalate, rethink, or hand to a fresh agent. For a stubborn or flaky bug, have separate agents form hypotheses from disjoint evidence (logs, code, data) and put each in front of a refuter; one context tends to favour its own first theory.
 
 Cheaper than strike three: use the active harness's checkpoint or restore mechanism, or git when appropriate, instead of stacking corrections onto polluted context. Preserve unrelated user edits; revert only your identified changes or retry in an isolated worktree. A clean retry usually beats a long session full of failed patches.
 
